## Practice Question 1: The Powder Factor Calculation (Module 3)
Scenario:
You are designing a single-row bench blast in a limestone quarry. Your drill hole diameter requires a Burden (B) of 3 meters and a Spacing (S) of 4 meters. The bench height (the vertical depth of the rock face) is 10 meters. You load each hole with 72 kilograms of an emulsion blasting agent.
Your Task:
Calculate the Powder Factor for this blast in kilograms per cubic meter ($\text{kg/m}^3$).
## Step-by-Step Solution:

   1. Calculate the Volume of Rock broken per hole ($V$):
   $$\text{Volume} = \text{Burden} \times \text{Spacing} \times \text{Bench Height}$$ 
   $$V = 3\text{ m} \times 4\text{ m} \times 10\text{ m} = 120\text{ m}^3$$ 
   2. Calculate the Powder Factor ($\text{PF}$):
   $$\text{Powder Factor} = \frac{\text{Total Weight of Explosive}}{\text{Volume of Rock Broken}}$$ 
   $$\text{PF} = \frac{72\text{ kg}}{120\text{ m}^3} = \mathbf{0.6\text{ kg/m}^3}$$ 

Exam Tip: In a real exam, if they ask for the powder factor in kg/ton, they will provide the density of the rock. You would simply multiply the volume ($120\text{ m}^3$) by the rock's density before dividing.
